\chapter{Groth16 zk-SNARK Verification}
\section{Introduction to Zero-Knowledge Verification}

Zero-knowledge succinct non-interactive arguments of knowledge (zk-SNARKs)
provide a cryptographic mechanism for proving the correctness of a computation
without revealing the underlying private data. The Groth16 proving system,
introduced by Jens Groth in 2016, remains one of the most efficient and widely
deployed zk-SNARK constructions due to its constant-size proofs and fast
verification time.

Within the BAAC-SE v1.0 architecture designed by independent systems architect
C.~Matthew Blackwell, Groth16 serves as the verification backbone that ensures
the correctness of approximate CKKS homomorphic computations. The bounded-error
residual witness architecture defined in Chapter 4 produces structured witness
variables that encode both ciphertext transitions and residual error bounds.
Groth16 then verifies these constraints through a pairing-based protocol over a
pairing-friendly elliptic curve.

\section{Rank-1 Constraint Systems (R1CS)}

Groth16 operates over a Rank-1 Constraint System (R1CS), which expresses the
entire computation as a set of multiplicative constraints of the form:
\[
\langle A_r, w \rangle \cdot \langle B_r, w \rangle
= \langle C_r, w \rangle,
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $A_r$, $B_r$, and $C_r$ are coefficient vectors,
    \item $w$ is the complete witness vector containing public inputs,
          private variables, and intermediate values,
    \item $r$ indexes each constraint row.
\end{itemize}

The bounded-error residual witness architecture integrates directly into this
framework by encoding:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item residual extraction constraints,
    \item boolean bit-decomposition constraints,
    \item reconstruction constraints for bounded coefficients,
    \item CKKS pipeline consistency constraints.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Quadratic Arithmetic Programs (QAP)}

To enable succinct proof generation, Groth16 compiles the R1CS into a Quadratic
Arithmetic Program (QAP). Each R1CS row is mapped to a polynomial identity over
an evaluation domain $\{t_1, t_2, \dots, t_m\}$:
\[
A(X) B(X) - C(X) = H(X) Z(X),
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $A(X)$, $B(X)$, and $C(X)$ are polynomial encodings of the constraint matrices,
    \item $Z(X)$ is the vanishing polynomial,
    \item $H(X)$ is the prover-generated quotient polynomial.
\end{itemize}

The correctness of the computation is equivalent to the divisibility of
$A(X)B(X) - C(X)$ by $Z(X)$. Groth16 uses bilinear pairings to verify this
relation succinctly.

\section{Verification Key Structure}

The Groth16 verification key (VK) contains:
\begin{itemize}
    \item commitments to the QAP polynomials evaluated at a secret point $\tau$,
    \item elliptic-curve elements encoding the constraint structure,
    \item public input commitments,
    \item pairing parameters for the target curve (e.g., BN254).
\end{itemize}

Within BAAC-SE v1.0, the VK is memory-mapped into the real-time execution
environment and loaded by the Core~2 verifier gatekeeper. This ensures that
every bionic actuation decision is cryptographically validated before motor
torque tensors are dispatched.

\section{Proof Generation and Verification}

The Groth16 prover constructs a proof $\pi$ consisting of three elliptic-curve
elements:
\[
\pi = (A, B, C),
\]
representing commitments to the witness and QAP quotient polynomial.

Verification is performed by checking a single pairing equation:
\[
e(A, B) = e(C, \gamma),
\]
where $\gamma$ is part of the verification key.

If the equality holds, the computation is proven correct. If not, the BAAC-SE
v1.0 fail-safe interlock is triggered, preventing unsafe bionic actuation.

\section{Integration with Bounded-Error Witness Architecture}

The bounded-error residual witness architecture ensures that approximate CKKS
computations remain verifiable within the Groth16 framework. The integration
pipeline includes:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Encoding residual coefficients and bit-decomposition arrays into the witness.
    \item Enforcing $\ell_\infty$ bounds through boolean and reconstruction constraints.
    \item Linking CKKS ciphertext transitions to R1CS rows.
    \item Generating a succinct proof that the entire approximate computation
          remains within safe numerical limits.
\end{enumerate}

This fusion of approximate homomorphic encryption with exact zero-knowledge
verification represents a novel cryptographic engineering achievement, enabling
real-time astrophysical-driven bionic control under strict safety guarantees.